Deleted:  The knee-flexor run quantifies what the mono-pulley freedom is worth at unity gain. As a uniarticular knee flexor, the biceps femoris short head is exactly the site the mono-pulley variant serves, and the run converged to a collision-feasible layout with zero bound violations, both actuators of the rig reeved over a single tackle of one line part, so the gain was 1 and no force multiplication was engaged; the run therefore measures the redirect baseline, not the transmission trade. Even the redirect alone deepened the peak predicted flexion torque from \qty{81.23}{
ewton\meter} on the direct route to \qty{85.74}{
ewton\meter} on the same knee-axis measure, \qty{85.56}{
ewton\meter} on the insertion-side measure after the tackle's force loss, a gain of \qty{4.51}{
ewton\meter} or \qty{5.6}{\percent}, while holding the off-axis torque to at most \qty{0.803}{
ewton\meter}, under \qty{1}{\percent} of the peak. The direct and pulley runs optimize different objective functions, so their objective values, 74.47 and 0.08465, are not commensurable; the torque comparison, not the objective comparison, is the meaningful one. That comparison should still be read as the value of the added routing freedom in this pair of runs rather than as an isolated pulley effect, since no run at fixed objective separates the pulley's contribution. The predicted peaks of the direct and pulley routes exceed the human flexor scale of \qty{23.16}{
ewton\meter} by factors of 3.51 and 3.70, and the torque-for-range exchange that a reeved tackle buys is quantified by the doctrine re-solves of the batch analysis that follows.
